#!/usr/bin/expect -f
#
# PROTOTYPE — throwaway. Drives pi's REAL TUI behind a pty and captures what it renders.
#
# Why this exists: every previous check of the #184 work ran in `--print`/JSON mode, where
# `ui.notify` and `ui.setStatus` are invisible by construction — they are the TUI channel. So the
# notice and the status line have never been observed in a real pi at all. This is the only rig
# that can see them.
#
# A prior attempt in this repo failed for a reason worth recording: a pty capture "showed neither
# the notice nor a control marker", because a TUI quits on stdin EOF before a toast renders. The
# fix is a driver that OWNS stdin (`expect`), rather than a pipe that closes.
#
#
# ── VERDICT (2026-10-12, real pi, DEFAULT fullscreen TUI, build is the only variable) ────────────
#
# Fullscreen has been the default since 1.0 ("Fullscreen by default", vendor CHANGELOG). An earlier
# pass drove `--tui-mode regular` — the NON-default renderer — so it was measuring the wrong surface.
#
#   scenario                  A local                       B installed 2.0.3
#   ------------------------  ---------------------------  -------------------------------------------
#   boot, empty registry      no notice, no status marker   notice in startup panel + status marker
#   configured, 1 definition  no notice, no status marker   no notice, no status marker
#   codemode, after a call    no status marker              status marker, row 45, warning colour
#   dispatch, no `agent`      tool call WITHOUT `agent`,    tool call forced to carry `agent`,
#                             fulfilled, PONG returned     refused: `must not have fewer than 1`
#
# Reads, one per row:
#
#   The `configured` row is what makes the `boot` row mean something. With one definition present
#   2.0.3 stops warning too, so the boot difference is SUPPRESSION — not a build that never warns.
#
#   #187 has TWO faces, rendered in two different places. The notice is a long
#   `Warning: pi-ptc-subagents: no registered agent was found…` line inside the STARTUP PANEL. The
#   status marker is a short `no registered agent` painted on the row BELOW the bottom bar — raw
#   bytes, present at boot and still present after codemode activation:
#   `ESC[45;1H ESC[2K ESC[38;5;172m no registered agent ESC[39m`.
#
#   The status half went unobserved for one reason, and it was the search term: `ptc-mode` is the
#   status KEY, and a key is never rendered. Grepping captures for it returns 0 on BOTH builds —
#   including 2.0.3 with the marker plainly on screen. Counting one marker while looking for the
#   other is the whole error. An earlier draft of this header claimed the boot scenario "cannot see
#   this face at all, the setter only runs on activation"; the measurement contradicts that — under
#   `-e builtin:codemode` 2.0.3 paints it at boot too.
#
#   #185 in the TUI: the local build's tool call renders as `ptc_subagent  task="reply PONG"` —
#   no `agent` key at all — and the child's PONG comes back. 2.0.3 cannot omit it, so the model
#   GUESSED `"agent": ""`, and the TUI shows pi rejecting it at the schema. That accidentally
#   exercised the branch #185 exists for: absent is legal, empty is a mistake, and the two must not
#   be conflated.
#
# The wrong turn worth keeping: "typing does not reach pi's TUI, it wants the kitty keyboard
# protocol" was WRONG. The kitty protocol is real — pi emits `ESC[?u` and pushes `ESC[>7u` under
# every TERM — but it was not the cause. The cause was one level up: a bare pty is not a terminal.
# Fullscreen mode asks the terminal for its colours (`OSC 10;?` / `OSC 11;?` / `OSC 4;N;?`), its
# identity (`ESC[c`) and its size, and a bare pty answers none — so the renderer waits, the editor
# is never drawn, and an undrawn editor has nowhere to receive input. Give the pty a viewport and
# answer those queries and text lands: measured ~1.5 KB → ~15 KB of capture, typed text on screen.
#
# Harness failures. Four of them, each of which first produced a confident wrong answer — the shape
# this repo keeps paying for: a check that reports a result about the thing it never observed.
#   - a fixed 14s wait, then 50s, left the startup panel owning the screen where input is swallowed.
#     Removed as a variable (`--offline`) rather than guessed at; 25s is the measured value.
#   - the first liveness check asked whether a string appeared ANYWHERE in the capture, wrong for a
#     raw stream — an early frame stays in the bytes. Ask whether what you SENT arrived.
#   - the check then grepped the capture FILE, which expect had not flushed: it read 0 three times
#     while the capture held the prompt four times and a running turn (`Working`, `1.4%/1.0M`), so
#     the script skipped its wait and tore a live dispatch down. Fixed by matching the incoming
#     stream instead — no file, no flush, no lag.
#   - the arrival needle was an UNESCAPED regex, so the codemode prompt containing `21*2` could
#     never match itself: three runs reported "landed: 0" while the capture plainly showed the
#     prompt in the input box and the tool call going through. A prompt that fails its own arrival
#     check is worse than no check — it turns a working run into a "could not drive it" claim.
#
# Usage:  expect prototypes/__tui-check.exp <scenario> <ext-path> <out-file>
#   scenario  boot | configured | dispatch | codemode
#   ext-path  a package dir (`-e <dir>`) — the local tree, or the installed 2.0.3
#
# Not production. Delete when answered.

set scenario  [lindex $argv 0]
set extPath   [lindex $argv 1]
set outFile   [lindex $argv 2]

set timeout 240

# An optional working directory. The notice's condition is "the registry is empty", and the
# registry's PROJECT half is answered from the session cwd — so pointing the session at a cwd that
# holds one definition is how to test the CONDITION rather than only its absence.
if {[info exists env(TUI_CWD)]} { cd $env(TUI_CWD) }
log_user 0
log_file -a $outFile
set env(TERM) "xterm-256color"

# `-ne` plus an explicit `-e builtin:codemode`: codemode activation is what makes surface
# detection register `ptc_subagent` at all, so without it neither build has a face to inspect.
# The pty must be a TERMINAL, not just a pipe. Two things were missing, and each was measured:
#
#   * a viewport — fullscreen mode reads terminal rows/cols;
#   * ANSWERS to the queries pi sends before it draws. `PI_TUI_WRITE_LOG` (the vendor's own
#     diagnostic for the raw ANSI stream) shows it asking for foreground/background colours
#     (`OSC 10;?` / `OSC 11;?`), the 16-colour palette (`OSC 4;N;?`), and DA1 (`ESC[c`). A bare pty
#     never replies, and fullscreen mode waits before laying out the editor.
#
# Measured, same build and same scenario: without the answers the capture is ~1.5 KB and typed
# text never lands; with them it is ~15 KB, the alternate screen is entered, and the text lands.
# Without an editor there is nowhere for input to go — which is the actual reason typing "did not
# work", not the keyboard protocol this was first blamed on.
set stty_init "rows 45 columns 140"

# `--offline`: startup's package-update check is a network call, and while the startup panel owns
# the screen typed input is swallowed — measured twice, at 14s and at 50s, so the wait was removed
# as a variable rather than guessed at.
spawn -noecho pi --offline -ne -e builtin:codemode -e $extPath \
    --provider deepseek --model deepseek-flash

# Bound this loop separately: pi sends all four queries within the first moments of startup, so
# after they are answered nothing else will ever match and the block would otherwise sit out the
# full global timeout. Measured cost of leaving it unbounded: ~240s dead per run, ~4min each.
set timeout 20
expect {
    -re "\x1b\\[c"               { send -- "\x1b\\[?62;22c"; exp_continue }
    -re "\x1b\\]10;\\?\x07"      { send -- "\x1b\\]10;rgb:ffff/ffff/ffff\x07"; exp_continue }
    -re "\x1b\\]11;\\?\x07"      { send -- "\x1b\\]11;rgb:0000/0000/0000\x07"; exp_continue }
    -re "\x1b\\]4;\\d+;\\?\x07"  { send -- "\x1b\\]4;0;rgb:0000/0000/0000\x07"; exp_continue }
    timeout { }
}
set timeout 240

# The startup screen is where a `session_start` notice lands, and the panel that owns the screen
# until it finishes swallows typed input. How long to wait has been probed three times — 14s, then
# 50s, then removing the variable with `--offline` — because startup does a network call whose
# latency moves the panel's exit. 25s is the value that carried the known-good baseline
# (`answer-probe.exp`, whose typed text reached the screen); overridable so it can be re-probed.
set bootWait 25
#
# Note what a SMALL capture does NOT mean: an idle TUI stops redrawing, so a capture that stops
# growing proves nothing either way — it is not evidence that pi died. The question to ask is
# whether the text ARRIVED, not whether bytes kept coming.
if {[info exists env(TUI_WAIT)]} { set bootWait $env(TUI_WAIT) }
sleep $bootWait

# Wait for the typed text to appear in what the TUI actually writes, WITHOUT going through the
# capture file.
#
# Two earlier checks tried the file and both were wrong. Grepping the file saw 0 three times while
# the capture in fact held the prompt four times and a running turn (`Working`, `1.4%/1.0M`) —
# expect's log_file had not flushed, so the check reported a result about the thing it was
# measuring while never having observed it. Forcing a flush via `log_file ""` does not work either
# (expect answers "cannot start logging without first stopping logging"). Matching on the incoming
# stream removes the whole class: no file, no flush, no lag.
proc awaitText {text seconds} {
    # The needle is matched as a REGEX, so it has to be escaped. Measured: the codemode prompt
    # contains `21*2`, and an unescaped `*` made the pattern mean something else — the run reported
    # "landed: 0" three times while the capture plainly showed the prompt sitting in the input box
    # and the tool call going through. A prompt that fails its own arrival check is worse than no
    # check: it silently converts a working run into a "could not drive it" claim.
    regsub -all {[\^\$\|\?\*\+\(\)\[\]\{\}\\.-]} $text {\\&} pattern
    set timeout $seconds
    expect {
        -re $pattern { return 1 }
        timeout { return 0 }
    }
}

if {$scenario eq "dispatch"} {
    # A dispatch with NO `agent` argument: the local build runs a generalist child, 2.0.3 refuses
    # it at the tool schema.
    #
    # Kept SHORT on purpose — the verification below greps this exact text out of the TUI's own
    # input-box render, and a long line would wrap and never match.
    set prompt "Use ptc_subagent with NO agent; task: reply PONG"
    set landed 0
    for {set attempt 1} {$attempt <= 3} {incr attempt} {
        send -- "$prompt\r"
        sleep 12
        # Self-verifying send: typing into a TUI whose startup panel still owns the screen is
        # silently swallowed, and the first run of this read as "the model chose not to dispatch"
        # rather than "nothing was ever typed".
        if {[awaitText "Use ptc_subagent with NO agent; task: reply PONG" 12]} { set landed 1; break }
        sleep 18
    }
    puts "  typed prompt landed: $landed (after $attempt attempt(s))"
    # Wait for the turn REGARDLESS. A successful submit starts a real turn that spawns a child pi;
    # tearing down early reports "no dispatch happened" for a dispatch that was still running.
    set afterSend 120
    if {[info exists env(TUI_AFTER)]} { set afterSend $env(TUI_AFTER) }
    if {$landed} { sleep $afterSend }
}

# The `codemode` scenario exists because #187 has TWO faces and only one of them had ever been
# observed. Reading 2.0.3's bundle says why the other one is invisible at boot:
#
#   let S = () => { ... ui.setStatus("ptc-mode", parts.join("  ") || undefined) }
#   let C = (...) => { enabled = true; ...; S(warning) }
#
# So the status key is `ptc-mode` but the PAINTED text is `PTC` + the warning — `ptc-mode` is an
# internal key and never appears on screen, which is why grepping the capture for it returned 0 on
# both builds and looked like "the marker was never removed". Worse, `S` only runs inside `C`,
# which is codemode ACTIVATION: a boot capture cannot see it by construction. Loading the codemode
# extension with `-e builtin:codemode` is not activation.
#
# Activation needs a `ptc_run_code` call. So this scenario types one, waits for the turn to finish,
# and then the capture can be read for `PTC`.
if {$scenario eq "codemode"} {
    set prompt "Use ptc_run_code to compute 21*2 and print the result"
    set landed 0
    for {set attempt 1} {$attempt <= 3} {incr attempt} {
        send -- "$prompt\r"
        if {[awaitText "Use ptc_run_code to compute 21*2 and print the result" 12]} { set landed 1; break }
        sleep 18
    }
    puts "  typed prompt landed: $landed (after $attempt attempt(s))"
    set afterSend 100
    if {[info exists env(TUI_AFTER)]} { set afterSend $env(TUI_AFTER) }
    if {$landed} { sleep $afterSend }
}

# Quit: the startup help advertises ctrl+c/ctrl+d as clear/exit. Send both, with a second round
# in case the first is swallowed by a dialog.
#
# The global timeout is 240s and it also governs THIS block, so a pi that does not exit parks the
# whole run for four minutes after the capture is already complete and the verdict already
# readable. Bound the teardown separately — measured cost of getting this wrong: one run reported
# nothing for 240s and had to be killed even though its capture was finished.
set timeout 12
send "\003"
sleep 3
send "\004"
sleep 3
send "\003"

expect {
    eof { }
    timeout {
        send "\003"
        sleep 2
    }
}
